<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-vectors-geometric-decompose" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Decomposing vectors in <m>\R^2</m> in terms of standard basis vectors</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case,
	express the vector <m>\uvec{v}</m>
	as a <term><xref ref="definition-vectors-linear-combination" text="custom">linear combination</xref></term>
	of the <term><xref ref="definition-vectors-std-basis" text="title"/></term> <m>\uvec{e}_1</m> and <m>\uvec{e}_2</m>.
	Then draw a vector addition triangle in the Cartesian plane that represents your decomposition.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p> <m> \uvec{v} = (3,4) </m> </p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p><m> (3,4) = 3 (1,0) + 4 (0,1) </m></p>

		<image width="35.2%">
			<latex-image label="image-vectors-exercises-geometric-decompose-answer-1">
				<xi:include href="geometric-decompose.d/answer-1.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a decomposition relative to the standard basis vectors in two dimensions. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of a right triangle in the <m>xy</m>-plane,
				with a directed line segment for each side.
				One leg of the triangle is a vector lying <em>along</em> the horizontal axis,
				extending from the origin to the point <m>P(3,0)</m>.
				This vector is labelled <m>3 \uvec{e}_1</m>.
				The other leg of the triangle is a vector running <em>parallel</em> to the vertical axis,
				extending from <m>P</m> to the point <m>Q(3,4)</m>.
				This vector is labelled <m>4 \uvec{e}_2</m>.
				The hypotenuse of the triangle is a vector labelled <m>\uvec{v}</m>,
				extending from the origin to <m>Q</m>.
			</p></description>
		</image>

	</answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p> <m> \uvec{v} = (5,-2) </m> </p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p><m> (5,-2) = 5 (1,0) + (-2) (0,1) </m></p>

		<image width="55.8%">
			<latex-image label="image-vectors-exercises-geometric-decompose-answer-2">
				<xi:include href="geometric-decompose.d/answer-2.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a decomposition relative to the standard basis vectors in two dimensions. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of a right triangle in the <m>xy</m>-plane,
				with a directed line segment for each side.
				One leg of the triangle is a vector lying <em>along</em> the horizontal axis,
				extending from the origin to the point <m>P(5,0)</m>.
				This vector is labelled <m>3 \uvec{e}_1</m>.
				The other leg of the triangle is a vector running <em>parallel</em> to the vertical axis,
				extending from <m>P</m> to the point <m>Q(5,-2)</m>.
				This vector is labelled <m>-2 \uvec{e}_2</m>.
				The hypotenuse of the triangle is a vector labelled <m>\uvec{v}</m>,
				extending from the origin to <m>Q</m>.
			</p></description>
		</image>

	</answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p> <m> \uvec{v} = (-3,1) </m> </p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p><m> (-3,1) = (-3) (1,0) + 1 (0,1) </m></p>

		<image width="40.2%">
			<latex-image label="image-vectors-exercises-geometric-decompose-answer-3">
				<xi:include href="geometric-decompose.d/answer-3.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a decomposition relative to the standard basis vectors in two dimensions. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of a right triangle in the <m>xy</m>-plane,
				with a directed line segment for each side.
				One leg of the triangle is a vector lying <em>along</em> the horizontal axis,
				extending from the origin to the point <m>P(-3,0)</m>.
				This vector is labelled <m>-3 \uvec{e}_1</m>.
				The other leg of the triangle is a vector running <em>parallel</em> to the vertical axis,
				extending from <m>P</m> to the point <m>Q(-3,1)</m>.
				This vector is labelled <m>\uvec{e}_2</m>.
				The hypotenuse of the triangle is a vector labelled <m>\uvec{v}</m>,
				extending from the origin to <m>Q</m>.
			</p></description>
		</image>

	</answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p> <m> \uvec{v} = \left(- \dfrac{3}{2}, - \dfrac{7}{8} \right) </m> </p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p><m> \left( - \dfrac{3}{2}, - \dfrac{7}{8} \right) = \left(- \frac{3}{2}\right) (1,0) + \left(- \frac{7}{8}\right) (0,1) </m></p>

		<image width="43.2%">
			<latex-image label="image-vectors-exercises-geometric-decompose-answer-4">
				<xi:include href="geometric-decompose.d/answer-4.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a decomposition relative to the standard basis vectors in two dimensions. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of a right triangle in the <m>xy</m>-plane,
				with a directed line segment for each side.
				One leg of the triangle is a vector lying <em>along</em> the horizontal axis,
				extending from the origin to the point <m>P(-\frac{3}{2},0)</m>.
				This vector is labelled <m>-\frac{3}{2} \uvec{e}_1</m>.
				The other leg of the triangle is a vector running <em>parallel</em> to the vertical axis,
				extending from <m>P</m> to the point <m>Q(-\frac{3}{2},-\frac{7}{8})</m>.
				This vector is labelled <m>-\frac{7}{8} \uvec{e}_2</m>.
				The hypotenuse of the triangle is a vector labelled <m>\uvec{v}</m>,
				extending from the origin to <m>Q</m>.
			</p></description>
		</image>

	</answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
